\section{Problem and protocol}\label{sec:protocol}
\paragraph{Setting.}
A history matrix $X\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times N}$ holds the scores of $M$ models on $N$ benchmarks with no missing cell (a dense pool; Section~\ref{sec:data}). A new model has an unobserved row $x\in\mathbb{R}^N$. The evaluator picks a set $S$ of $k$ benchmarks, runs the model on them, and predicts its scores on a set of target benchmarks $T$ disjoint from $S$. The selector and the predictor may use $X$ and the observed $x_S$, and nothing else about the new model.

\paragraph{Targets, splits and leakage.}
In the main design a target set $T$ of 30\% of the benchmarks is drawn at random, $S$ is chosen among the other 70\%, and the procedure is repeated over five random partitions per fold; every selector, predictor and $k$ is therefore scored on identical targets. (The literal problem, $T=[N]\setminus S$, lets the targets depend on the selector; it is a secondary design, Appendix~\ref{app:protocol}.) The main split is a grouped 10-fold split that holds out whole model \emph{families}, so no sibling of a test model is in the history; the family is the organisation plus the first two name tokens, and organisation-level families are a robustness check. Secondary splits are a random 10-fold split, which shares families between history and test, and a time-forward split that trains on the models released before a cut (55, 70 and 85\% of the release order) and tests on the next block. In every fold, the standardisation (training mean and standard deviation per benchmark), the transform, the rank and the parameters of the factor model, the selection and the predictor's tuning use only the training rows.

